From 88d921ec5021d7109e9bfc21525c9f60d07f9942 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <opus5@local>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 00:50:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cuda/hip: fold the MoE routing-weight multiply into the
 routed down projection (+1.69% decode, bit-exact)

Decode census on the 23-patch frontier build: 778 dispatches per token for
4.96 ms of kernel inside a 6.07 ms wall token, so kernel is 82% of decode and
every removed node is worth its own kernel time plus ~1.43 us of gap. Exactly
two `k_bin_bcast` families survive in that census, and the larger one is one
launch per layer applying the MoE routing weights:

    experts = ggml_mul(experts[n_embd, n_expert_used], weights[1, n_expert_used])

39 launches per token, 1.63 us each, moving the whole expert output through
memory to apply EIGHT scalars. The matvec that produced those bytes already
holds the finished value in a register and already knows which destination
channel - i.e. which expert slot, i.e. which weight - it is writing.

`dst_scale` is added to the mm-fusion argument struct: one f32 per destination
channel, applied to `result` immediately before the store, with the store
redirected to the MUL's destination. One scalar load per block.

Two things make this a different patch here than on the qwen3.6 sibling:

- **It has to compose with the shared expert riding as a ninth channel**
  (0017). That channel is not routed and must not be scaled, so the multiply is
  predicated on `!is_extra` and the extra channel keeps its stock store. Both
  folds therefore ride the same launch: the census below shows the down
  projection still at `gy=9`.
- **The MUL is not adjacent to the matvec.** `build_moe_ffn` pins the shared
  expert's down projection between them so the backend can pair the two
  matvecs, so detection accepts the MUL at i+1 *or* i+2 and, in the second
  case, requires the intervening node to be that MUL_MAT and lists it as an
  output of the span. `ggml_can_fuse_subgraph` still proves nothing else reads
  the unscaled result.

`has_dst_scale` is deliberately NOT routed through the existing `has_fusion`
template flag: that flag also instantiates the gate path, whose
`tmp_shared_gate` doubles the block's LDS footprint on exactly this launch.

Bit-exact by construction - the multiplicand is the same rounded f32 the stock
kernel would have written and the MUL would have read back - and measurably so.

Detection is gated on `ne[2] == 1` (the decode shape), a contiguous f32 factor
of shape [1, n_expert_used], identical layout between the MUL's destination and
the matvec's, and `ggml_cuda_should_fuse_mul_mat_vec_q` so the node was taking
mmvq anyway. Prefill takes MMQ and is untouched, and measured neutral.
`GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_MOE_WEIGHT_FUSE=1` restores the stock pair.

Measured on the runner box (HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0), same binary via the env
toggle, whole-process launches, arm order alternated per round, 5 rounds:

| round | order | tg128 off | tg128 on | ratio |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|
| 1 | off,on | 164.48 | 167.26 | 1.0169 |
| 2 | on,off | 164.02 | 166.81 | 1.0170 |
| 3 | off,on | 163.77 | 166.21 | 1.0149 |
| 4 | on,off | 163.20 | 166.31 | 1.0191 |
| 5 | off,on | 162.98 | 165.58 | 1.0160 |

Median per-round ratio **1.0169**, 5/5 rounds positive and the two arms are
fully disjoint: min(on) 165.58 > max(off) 164.48. Both arms drift down together
across rounds (thermal), which is why the statistic is the per-round ratio.
pp534 median ratio 1.0041, i.e. neutral as designed.

- census, same binary, fold on vs off: **786.9 -> 747.9 dispatches/token,
  exactly -39.0**, and the entire `k_bin_bcast` y=8 population (39/token,
  68.8 us/token) is gone. Nothing else moved; the down projection keeps its
  `gy=9` shared-expert channel.
- perplexity, `fixtures/gainz-corpus.txt`, 8 chunks: BIT-IDENTICAL on both
  arms at both shapes - **19.5388** at the decode shape `-b 512 -ub 1`, which
  is the shape the fold actually fires at, and 19.6616 at the `-c 512` gate
  shape, where it correctly does not fire.

Do not read a kernel-time delta off two separately profiled processes here:
the first-profiled arm shows `topk_moe` and `rope_neox_grouped` - kernels
neither arm modifies - inflated 2.6x by cold clocks. Dispatch counts are
deterministic; wall time needs the interleaved A/B.
